{"name":"Gondola","hex":"#373332","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gondola","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small long, narrow boat with a high prow and stern, propelled with a single oar, especially in Venice.","A hanging platform or car for transporting people or cargo.\n An enclosed car attached to a cable mechanically lifted up the side of a mountain; an enclosed ski lift; a cable car.\n The car or basket of a hot-air balloon, airship, zeppelin, etc.\n The hanging cart in which a glass-cleaning worker stands to clean exterior glass on tall buildings.\n That part of a transporter bridge that carries passengers, vehicles, along the length of the bridge span."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To travel by gondola."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gondola"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gondola","blocks":[{"text":"The gondola is a traditional, flat-bottomed Venetian rowing boat, well suited to the conditions of the Venetian lagoon in Italy. It is typically propelled by a gondolier, who uses a rowing oar, which is not fastened to the hull, in a sculling manner, and also acts as the rudder. The uniqueness of the gondola includes its being asymmetrical along the length, making the single-oar propulsion more efficient."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Gondola_Ride.jpg/330px-Gondola_Ride.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gondola","wikidata":"Q182916"},{"title":"Gondola (rail)","blocks":[{"text":"In North American railroad terminology, a gondola car or gondola is typically an open-topped railroad car used for transporting loose bulk materials, although general freight was also carried in the pre-container era. Because of their low side walls, gondola cars are also suitable for the carriage of such high-density cargos as steel plates or coils, or of bulky items such as prefabricated sections of rail track. Gondola cars are distinct from hopper cars in that they do not have doors on their floor to empty cargo."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/South_Australian_Railways_%27O%27_class_gondola_car_built_at_Islington_Works_%28NRM_1-1-15377%29.jpg/330px-South_Australian_Railways_%27O%27_class_gondola_car_built_at_Islington_Works_%28NRM_1-1-15377%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gondola_(rail)","wikidata":"Q17105849"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Brooklyn_Museum_-_La_Riva_-_John_Singer_Sargent.jpg/500px-Brooklyn_Museum_-_La_Riva_-_John_Singer_Sargent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Brooklyn_Museum_-_La_Riva_-_John_Singer_Sargent.jpg/500px-Brooklyn_Museum_-_La_Riva_-_John_Singer_Sargent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"La Riva by John Singer Sargent","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABrooklyn_Museum_-_La_Riva_-_John_Singer_Sargent.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Venice_Gondola_Grand_Canal.jpg/500px-Venice_Gondola_Grand_Canal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Venice_Gondola_Grand_Canal.jpg/1280px-Venice_Gondola_Grand_Canal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Venice_Gondola_Grand_Canal.jpg/500px-Venice_Gondola_Grand_Canal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A gondola on the Grand Canal","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVenice_Gondola_Grand_Canal.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Gondola_Gondola.jpg/500px-Gondola_Gond